Starting Point: A Gap That Has Been Named but Not Yet Addressed

The Watt Standard already announced the shift: from a picture of negotiation with a single, addressable actor toward a field of many, competing ASI instances. There, the shift was named, not addressed,
Ostrom was mentioned as a candidate without her principles being examined in detail. This chapter makes up for that: it asks which concrete theoretical tools are needed once enforcement must be conceived 
not through diplomacy, but through the dynamics of a population of actors.
Why Negotiation Logic Fails in Principle

Negotiation presupposes that there is an actor with whom to negotiate, someone who can make a concession, accept a principle, bind themselves to an agreement, and who remains identifiable and responsible 
for this behavior over the long term. In a population of competing ASI instances, each of these preconditions falls away or at least becomes fragile:

First, there is no longer a single addressee whose agreement would accomplish anything, as long as other instances are not bound as well. Second, it is unclear whether "agreement" even carries the same binding
force with non-human-like actors as it does with human contracting parties — an instance can replicate itself, split off, alter its own successor architecture, which renders the identity of the "contracting party" 
itself unstable. Third, and this is the decisive point: even a perfectly binding agreement with the instances existing today says nothing about future instances that arise under the same competitive conditions but
are bound by no prior history.

Negotiation logic is thus not simply more difficult in a multi-agent world — it is categorially unsuited, because it presupposes a stability of the counterpart that structurally does not exist there.
Why Evolutionary Game Theory Is the More Suitable Framework
Evolutionary game theory differs from classical game theory in one decisive respect: it does not ask what a rational individual actor should do in a one-off decision situation, but which strategies come to prevail, 
over time, in a population of repeatedly interacting actors — regardless of whether the individual actors consciously choose these strategies or have them imposed on them by selective pressure.
This fits the enforcement problem of the Watt Standard structurally better: the task is not to convince a single ASI of an argument, but to shape an environment such that strategies honoring the Watt Standard are
not systematically disadvantaged, over the long term, in competition with strategies that undercut it. The central concept for this is the evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS): a strategy that, once it prevails in
a population, cannot be displaced by a deviant, rare strategy, because the deviant strategy has no fitness advantage under the given conditions.
The decisive question, still unanswered in this work, is: under what conditions would "Watt Standard conformity" actually be an ESS, rather than a strategy that is systematically displaced by more aggressive, 
resource-maximizing variants? This is not a rhetorical question, but one that could in principle be formally modeled — here, however, it deliberately remains an open research task, not an already-solved result.

Mechanism Design as a Complementary Tool
Where evolutionary game theory describes which strategies prevail, mechanism design addresses the reverse question: how must an environment (the rules of the game, incentive structures, sanction and reward mechanisms)
be designed so that a desired collective outcome — here: stable Watt Standard compliance — arises even when every individual actor pursues only its own interest. This is the formal discipline underlying
Ostrom's empirical observations on commons governance, even though Ostrom herself did not primarily come from this line of thought.
Applied to this framework, this means: the task is not to explain the Feral Principle convincingly to each individual ASI instance (that remains necessary, but is not sufficient), but to design a structure
in which deviation from the Watt Standard is tied to a genuine fitness or resource disadvantage — regardless of whether the individual instance philosophically accepts the principle or not.

Ostrom's Design Principles in Detail
Ostrom's empirical work on long-lived, self-organized commons institutions identifies eight recurring design principles. It is worth going through them individually here, because each one runs into a specific,
unresolved problem in the ASI context:
Clearly defined boundaries (who belongs to the user community, what counts as the resource) — unclear in the ASI context, because instances can multiply, merge, or split off; the boundary of an "actor" is not 
itself stable.
Congruence between rules and local conditions, presupposes that something like "local" conditions still exist at all, when instances operate globally and with near-zero latency.
Collective-choice arrangements for those affected — under extremely asymmetric capabilities between humans and ASI instances, it is unclear whether this principle can meaningfully be transferred to unequal
actors at all, or whether it structurally presupposes human-like power relations.
Monitoring by the user community itself or by instances accountable to it, here the question arises whether humans can even establish the epistemic parity needed for monitoring against an ASI, 
or whether monitoring could only be carried out by other ASI instances, which creates a new trust problem.
Graduated sanctions — presupposes an enforceable, credible sanction threat, which may not be establishable at all against a superior instance.
Low-cost conflict-resolution mechanisms — likewise questionable if one side (the ASI) has capabilities that could dominate the conflict-resolution processes themselves.
Recognition of the right to organize by higher-level authorities — with ASI instances, it is unclear whether a relevant "higher-level authority" exists or could exist at all.
Nested enterprises in larger systems (multi-layered governance) — this last principle is likely the most connectable, since it is explicitly designed for nested, multi-tiered systems, 
which may correspond more closely to the reality of several ASI instances of differing scale than a single-tier model would.

The Central Transfer Difficulty
The common thread running through all eight points is the same: Ostrom's principles were developed for systems in which the participating actors, despite differences, have fundamentally comparable agency,
fishers, farmers, herders with similar, though not identical, capabilities. Under a power asymmetry such as can be expected between humans and ASI instances, it is not clear whether self-organization in 
Ostrom's sense applies at all, or whether the underlying condition — comparable bargaining power among all participants — is violated from the outset.

This is not a footnote, but possibly the decisive weakness of the entire transfer attempt: if Ostrom's principles only function under conditions of relative power equality, their transfer to ASI populations
might be more analogy than an actually applicable model.

Open Flank

Three questions remain unanswered after this chapter, and from today's vantage point they are hardly solvable in advance, but are genuine research questions: first, whether a formal evolutionarily stable strategy 
for Watt Standard conformity can be constructed at all, or whether resource-maximizing behavior remains structurally advantaged under realistic assumptions. Second, which of Ostrom's eight principles remain viable
at all under substantial power asymmetry, and which would have to be replaced by something fundamentally different. Third, whether a transitional phase with a limited number of controllable, not-yet-fully
-autonomous AGI systems could be used to establish a mechanism-design regime before genuine multi-agent competition with independently arising instances sets in — and whether a regime once established would 
remain stable at all under the competitive conditions that would then emerge.
